=== VERDICT for hunt item 5: the hypothesis is REFUTED for the kernel. ===
  'A rejected request may still have had an effect' does not hold here, and neither does
  'silently lost'. The 200 {"accepted":1,"status":"queued"} is an ASYNC ACCEPT, and the
  endpoint it names keeps its promise: /ndt/get_flow_dispatch_status reported
    counters: dispatched 3, succeeded 1, failed 2
  and listed BOTH failures with dpid, match, op, timestamp and reason. The one install that
  should have worked (A, live switch) is the one that succeeded, and it landed on the switch.
  Residual, and it is the proxy's not the kernel's: the P4 proxy answers
  HTTP 200 with body {"status":"error","message":"Failed to add route"}. The kernel
  detects it explicitly ('reported an error in a 200 response'), so nothing is lost -- but a
  200 carrying an error is a trap for any other client.
  Observation, not yet a claim: an operator-installed rule does not survive a power cycle
  (10.99.99.99 was gone after s5 came back) and the power-on 200 does not mention it. The twin
  does not claim otherwise, so this is a documentation/expectation gap, not a divergence.
